      "sequence_position": 13,
      "problem": "Suppose $n=-8{p}^{4}+10{p}^{2}+4,$ where $p\\in Z.$ Complete the following argument.\n\nThe number $n$ is {{field-1}} since \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =-8{p}^{4}+10{p}^{2}+4 \\\\  & =2(-4{p}^{4}+{{field-2}}) \\\\  & ={{field-3}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a=${{field-4}} is an integer.",
      "worked_solution": "An integer is *even* if it can be written as $2a,$ where $a$ is an integer.\n\nNotice that all terms in the expression $-8{p}^{4}+10{p}^{2}+4$ have a common factor of $2.$ \n\nTherefore, we complete our argument as follows:\n\nThe number $n$ is $\\text{even}$ since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =-8{p}^{4}+10{p}^{2}+4 \\\\  & =2({{-4{p}^{4}+5{p}^{2}+2}_{⏟}}_{a}) \\\\  & =2a,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a=-4{p}^{4}+5{p}^{2}+2$ is an integer.",
